Semi-realistic 3D medical-illustration still of ONE human Type IIx skeletal muscle fibre (fast glycolytic fibre), centered in a square 1:1 1080x1080 frame with generous margin on a clean seamless dark studio background that fills the frame edge to edge. NO border, frame, mat, vignette or letterbox bar, and NOT rendered as a picture lying on a surface. Soft global illumination, gentle rim light, subtle subsurface scattering through the translucent sarcolemma, scientific-animation look.

CRITICAL FRAMING FIX: the fibre's two ends must be CUT OFF by the canvas edges. Do NOT draw a rounded end cap, a full round cross-section disc, or any visible tip at either end of the fibre. The cylinder must run diagonally corner to corner and appear to CONTINUE beyond the frame in both directions, as if the picture were a cropped window onto the middle of a much longer object, like a sliced sausage seen through a rectangular hole, never a complete capsule floating with both ends visible inside the canvas. CRITICAL FRAMING CHECK: at BOTH ends, the fibre's own body colour must fully cover the picture right into the corner it exits through, touching BOTH edges that meet at that corner with no background-coloured triangle of empty space left over — imagine the square canvas was itself cut out of a much bigger picture of a long fibre, so the fibre's colour runs straight off two full edges (not just close to them) at each end. A flat cut face, a rounded tip, or ANY margin of background between the fibre and an edge at either end is a FAIL — verify mentally that the four corners are: fibre-colour, background, background, fibre-colour (diagonal composition) before finishing. MORPHOLOGY (the most important instruction): a long, thick, perfectly UNBRANCHED cylindrical fibre running diagonally across the whole frame, cropped by the frame edge at BOTH ends so its true great length reads even though it cannot fully fit — it never forks, tapers into a Y, or sits as a short self-contained segment. It reads clearly as the WIDEST, largest-diameter fibre of the three human fibre types, and its overall colour, rendered in NATURAL, believable biological tones, is a PALE, translucent PINK-CREAM — distinctly lighter than a deep-red oxidative fibre, but never grey, neon, or colourless.

The fibre's surface carries two distinct patterns at once, both real and both must be visible together: (1) regular narrow bands running ACROSS the fibre, perpendicular to its long axis, repeating evenly end to end like rungs on a ladder or the rings of a hose — a slightly deeper dusty-rose A band alternates with a pale cream I band, with a fine slate-blue Z-disc line at the centre of each I band, aligned in register, and this transverse banding continues the ENTIRE visible length with no smooth gaps; (2) underneath that transverse banding, fine parallel MYOFIBRIL striping runs lengthwise along the fibre's long axis, giving the surface a combed, ribbed grain within each transverse band. Both patterns are visible at once — the transverse ladder-rung bands crossing the fibre, and the fine lengthwise myofibril grain inside each band — the way woven fabric shows both a weft and a warp.

A soft translucent cutaway running along part of the fibre reveals the interior:
- the parallel MYOFIBRILS carrying that same transverse striation pattern;
- along the fibre's outer rim, spaced out one after another, AT LEAST SEVEN separate oval, flattened, softly glowing blue-violet NUCLEI, every one modelled immediately under the translucent sarcolemma at the periphery — never a single nucleus in the middle;
- small amber-ochre MITOCHONDRIA modelled SPARSELY and individually between the myofibrils, deliberately few, small, and spaced apart rather than densely packed;
- abundant tiny round golden-tan GLYCOGEN GRANULES dusted through the sarcoplasm between the myofibrils;
- alongside the fibre, outside the sarcolemma, one slender CAPILLARY with a fine crimson lumen tracking part of the fibre's length, clearly thin and sparse.

DO NOT model: any branching, forking or Y-shape; intercalated discs; a short stubby segment fitting entirely inside the frame; a single central nucleus or any nucleus not touching the rim; deep red or grey/colourless tones; densely packed rows of large mitochondria; a smooth unstriated patch anywhere along the fibre; a cell wall, nucleoid, plasmids, flagella or cilia. Absolutely NO text, letters, numbers, labels, arrows, scale bars or watermarks anywhere in the image.
 The CAPILLARY must be rendered as a smooth, thin, continuously curving vessel of a single uniform dusty-rose/crimson tone throughout its length — like a fine blood vessel or thread. Do NOT render it as a candy-cane, barber-pole, segmented rod, swab stick, thermometer, or anything with alternating red-and-white stripes or beads; it must look organic and continuous, not like a manufactured object. Re-confirming: BOTH ends of the fibre must be sliced off by the picture edge — neither end may show a complete closed oval/round end-cap disc anywhere inside the canvas.
